<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annals and Antiquities of Rajasthan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1829">1829</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Annals and Antiquities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> is a history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-tod">James Tod</a>, published in two volumes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1829 and 1832. Drawing on bardic chronicles, inscriptions and his years as political agent in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a>, Tod gave pride of place to Mewar and portrayed the Rajputs as a chivalric, feudal society. The book shaped British and Indian views of Rajput history for a century, though historians now treat its romanticism with caution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annals-and-antiquities-of-rajasthan.jpg" alt="Annals and Antiquities of Rajasthan" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Painting contemporaneous (Tod died 1830s). Photograph taken before 1920 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colonel_James_Tod.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
